WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.050 line:85%
There’s a bottle in the fridge if you need it.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.051 line:85%
What would you verify before offering this bottle?

3
00:00:11.031 --> 00:00:13.071 line:85%
Pause here and name what you would check.

4
00:00:33.101 --> 00:00:34.329 line:85%
Here’s what to check.

5
00:00:35.309 --> 00:00:37.626 line:85%
First, which baby is this bottle for?

6
00:00:38.256 --> 00:00:41.831 line:85%
When milk is stored for more than one child, the label should say.

7
00:00:42.811 --> 00:00:44.360 line:85%
Second, what it contains.

8
00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:47.840 line:85%
Is it expressed breast milk or prepared formula?

9
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.667 line:85%
Third, its handling history.

10
00:00:51.297 --> 00:00:54.361 line:85%
When was the milk expressed, or the formula prepared?

11
00:00:54.991 --> 00:00:58.481 line:85%
Has it been frozen, thawed, warmed, or offered at a feed?

12
00:00:59.111 --> 00:01:01.790 line:85%
Those events decide whether it’s still usable.

13
00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:05.535 line:85%
The parent’s sentence tells you where the bottle is.

14
00:01:06.165 --> 00:01:08.481 line:85%
It doesn’t tell you whether it’s safe to offer.

15
00:01:09.111 --> 00:01:13.809 line:85%
In this workshop you’ll learn to read a bottle the way you’d read a chart: what’s

16
00:01:13.839 --> 00:01:17.252 line:85%
in it, what has happened to it, and which deadline applies.

17
00:01:18.232 --> 00:01:21.441 line:85%
We use CDC as our standard for storage times,

18
00:01:21.471 --> 00:01:25.327 line:85%
with guidance from the American Academy of Pediatrics.

19
00:01:25.957 --> 00:01:30.791 line:85%
This guidance is for routine care of healthy babies at home in the United States.

20
00:01:31.421 --> 00:01:37.020 line:85%
For a baby born prematurely, a baby in the hospital, or a baby with medical needs,

21
00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.903 line:85%
follow the plan from the baby’s care team.

22
00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:44.642 line:85%
Whenever formula is involved, follow the directions on the product.

23
00:01:45.622 --> 00:01:48.173 line:85%
Keep your Milk Handling Decision Sheet nearby.

24
00:01:48.803 --> 00:01:52.494 line:85%
The examples in this workshop are fictional teaching examples,

25
00:01:52.524 --> 00:01:56.155 line:85%
and you’ll use the sheet for a 2:00 a.m. decision at the end.

26
00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.353 line:85%
Start clean.

27
00:01:59.983 --> 00:02:04.830 line:85%
CDC says to wash your hands well with soap and water before expressing

28
00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.502 line:85%
or handling breast milk.

29
00:02:07.132 --> 00:02:09.507 line:85%
If soap and water aren’t available,

30
00:02:09.537 --> 00:02:14.867 line:85%
use an alcohol based hand sanitizer that contains at least 60 percent alcohol.

31
00:02:15.847 --> 00:02:19.380 line:85%
Store milk in breast milk storage bags, or in clean,

32
00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:24.244 line:85%
food grade containers made of glass or plastic with tight fitting lids.

33
00:02:24.874 --> 00:02:27.132 line:85%
Never use disposable bottle liners,

34
00:02:27.162 --> 00:02:30.924 line:85%
or plastic bags that aren’t meant for storing breast milk.

35
00:02:31.904 --> 00:02:35.842 line:85%
Label each container clearly with the date the milk was expressed.

36
00:02:36.472 --> 00:02:39.759 line:85%
If the milk goes to a childcare provider, CDC

37
00:02:39.789 --> 00:02:43.077 line:85%
and the AAP also say to add the child’s name.

38
00:02:44.057 --> 00:02:48.435 line:85%
In the Ivory Cradle workflow, we also write the time it was expressed,

39
00:02:48.465 --> 00:02:51.899 line:85%
and any later event, such as when it was moved to thaw.

40
00:02:52.529 --> 00:02:56.296 line:85%
That’s our teaching practice, so the next caregiver doesn’t have to guess.

41
00:02:57.276 --> 00:02:58.206 line:85%
Practice.

42
00:02:58.836 --> 00:03:03.542 line:85%
The parent filled this bag on Sunday at 9:15 p.m. for her daughter, Maya.

43
00:03:04.172 --> 00:03:05.571 line:85%
What goes on the label?

44
00:03:06.201 --> 00:03:07.430 line:85%
Pause and write it.

45
00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.798 line:85%
The date it was expressed is required.

46
00:03:30.428 --> 00:03:33.470 line:85%
Maya’s name matters whenever milk could be mixed up.

47
00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:36.033 line:85%
The time is our workflow habit.

48
00:03:37.013 --> 00:03:39.585 line:85%
Now, how long fresh milk keeps, and where.

49
00:03:40.565 --> 00:03:46.439 line:85%
Freshly expressed milk can stay at room temperature, 77°F or colder,

50
00:03:46.469 --> 00:03:48.002 line:85%
for up to 4 hours.

51
00:03:48.982 --> 00:03:54.968 line:85%
In the refrigerator, at 40°F or colder, it can be stored for up to 4 days.

52
00:03:55.948 --> 00:04:00.729 line:85%
In the freezer, at 0°F or colder, about 6 months is best,

53
00:04:00.759 --> 00:04:03.598 line:85%
and up to 12 months is acceptable.

54
00:04:04.578 --> 00:04:08.964 line:85%
Store milk toward the back of the refrigerator or freezer, not in the door.

55
00:04:09.594 --> 00:04:14.569 line:85%
Further inside, it’s protected from temperature changes when the door opens

56
00:04:14.599 --> 00:04:15.303 line:85%
and closes.

57
00:04:16.283 --> 00:04:19.586 line:85%
If you don’t think the milk will be used within 4 days,

58
00:04:19.616 --> 00:04:21.586 line:85%
CDC says to freeze it right away.

59
00:04:22.216 --> 00:04:26.751 line:85%
And when you take milk out, use the oldest first: first in, first out.

60
00:04:27.731 --> 00:04:30.535 line:85%
You may see different numbers in AAP materials,

61
00:04:30.565 --> 00:04:33.248 line:85%
such as up to 9 months in a standard freezer.

62
00:04:33.878 --> 00:04:39.191 line:85%
This course teaches the CDC times, so every answer in the video, the worksheet,

63
00:04:39.221 --> 00:04:40.611 line:85%
and the quiz matches.

64
00:04:41.241 --> 00:04:46.267 line:85%
If the family’s pediatrician gives different instructions, follow the family’s plan.

65
00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:52.464 line:85%
When milk will be frozen, store small amounts, 2 to 4 ounces

66
00:04:52.494 --> 00:04:56.193 line:85%
or the amount offered at one feeding, so less is wasted.

67
00:04:57.173 --> 00:05:00.756 line:85%
Leave about one inch of space at the top of the container,

68
00:05:00.786 --> 00:05:03.372 line:85%
because breast milk expands as it freezes.

69
00:05:04.002 --> 00:05:06.660 line:85%
Keep the oldest bags where they’ll be used first.

70
00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:12.610 line:85%
To thaw milk, put the container in the refrigerator overnight, set it in warm

71
00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:16.442 line:85%
or lukewarm water, or hold it under lukewarm running water.

72
00:05:17.072 --> 00:05:20.242 line:85%
Never thaw or heat breast milk in a microwave.

73
00:05:21.222 --> 00:05:22.664 line:85%
Here’s a worked example.

74
00:05:23.294 --> 00:05:27.957 line:85%
On Monday at 8:00 p.m., a frozen bag is moved to the refrigerator.

75
00:05:28.587 --> 00:05:31.864 line:85%
By Tuesday at 6:00 a.m., it has completely thawed.

76
00:05:32.494 --> 00:05:34.085 line:85%
Which event starts the clock?

77
00:05:54.115 --> 00:05:58.890 line:85%
CDC says to start counting 24 hours when the milk is completely thawed,

78
00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.936 line:85%
not from the time it came out of the freezer.

79
00:06:02.566 --> 00:06:05.843 line:85%
So this bag should be used by Wednesday at 6:00 a.m.

80
00:06:06.823 --> 00:06:08.713 line:85%
Write the thawed time on the label.

81
00:06:09.343 --> 00:06:13.323 line:85%
If you can’t tell when it finished thawing, ask the parent before you use it.

82
00:06:14.303 --> 00:06:19.287 line:85%
Once thawed milk is brought to room temperature or warmed, use it within 2 hours.

83
00:06:19.917 --> 00:06:22.703 line:85%
And never refreeze breast milk after it has thawed.

84
00:06:23.683 --> 00:06:29.075 line:85%
For travel between homes, breast milk can be kept in an insulated cooler with

85
00:06:29.105 --> 00:06:31.610 line:85%
frozen ice packs for up to 24 hours.

86
00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:37.202 line:85%
At the destination, use it right away, store it in the refrigerator, or freeze it.

87
00:06:38.182 --> 00:06:41.845 line:85%
Frozen milk that’s moved to another freezer should go quickly,

88
00:06:41.875 --> 00:06:44.168 line:85%
packed on ice packs so it stays frozen.

89
00:06:44.798 --> 00:06:47.755 line:85%
Its age still counts from the first time it was frozen.

90
00:06:48.735 --> 00:06:50.902 line:85%
Now a brief power outage example.

91
00:06:51.532 --> 00:06:53.230 line:85%
The power was off overnight.

92
00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:56.283 line:85%
When it comes back on, you check two bags.

93
00:06:56.913 --> 00:06:58.846 line:85%
Bag A still has ice crystals.

94
00:06:59.476 --> 00:07:02.561 line:85%
Bag B has completely thawed but still feels cold.

95
00:07:03.191 --> 00:07:04.441 line:85%
What happens to each?

96
00:07:24.471 --> 00:07:30.591 line:85%
CDC says frozen milk that has started to thaw but still contains ice crystals can

97
00:07:30.621 --> 00:07:31.502 line:85%
be refrozen.

98
00:07:32.132 --> 00:07:34.107 line:85%
So Bag A can go back in the freezer.

99
00:07:35.087 --> 00:07:40.049 line:85%
Milk that has completely thawed but still feels cold goes in the refrigerator,

100
00:07:40.079 --> 00:07:42.993 line:85%
to be used within the next day or thrown away.

101
00:07:43.623 --> 00:07:44.745 line:85%
That’s Bag B.

102
00:07:45.375 --> 00:07:49.036 line:85%
Tell the parent what happened, so the family can decide what to keep.

103
00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:53.480 line:85%
Stored breast milk often separates.

104
00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:56.789 line:85%
The fat rises and forms a layer on top.

105
00:07:57.419 --> 00:07:58.371 line:85%
That’s normal.

106
00:07:59.001 --> 00:08:03.579 line:85%
CDC says to swirl the milk to mix the fat, which may have separated.

107
00:08:04.559 --> 00:08:07.771 line:85%
Families sometimes ask whether they can shake it instead.

108
00:08:08.401 --> 00:08:12.281 line:85%
The CDC and AAP pages we use recommend a gentle swirl,

109
00:08:12.311 --> 00:08:15.902 line:85%
and they don’t say that shaking makes milk unsafe.

110
00:08:16.532 --> 00:08:18.806 line:85%
In this course we demonstrate the swirl.

111
00:08:19.436 --> 00:08:22.755 line:85%
If a family prefers a different method, follow their plan.

112
00:08:23.735 --> 00:08:26.671 line:85%
What about adding fresh milk to milk that’s already chilled?

113
00:08:27.301 --> 00:08:32.266 line:85%
CDC advises against mixing freshly expressed milk with already cooled

114
00:08:32.296 --> 00:08:36.103 line:85%
or frozen milk, because it can rewarm the older milk.

115
00:08:36.733 --> 00:08:39.177 line:85%
Cool the fresh milk first, then combine.

116
00:08:40.157 --> 00:08:43.137 line:85%
When cooled milk from different times is combined,

117
00:08:43.167 --> 00:08:46.869 line:85%
base the storage time on when the older milk was first stored.

118
00:08:47.849 --> 00:08:48.779 line:85%
Practice.

119
00:08:49.409 --> 00:08:53.219 line:85%
Milk expressed Monday at 7:00 a.m. was chilled right away.

120
00:08:53.849 --> 00:08:58.320 line:85%
Milk expressed Monday at 7:00 p.m. is chilled, then combined with it.

121
00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:00.904 line:85%
Which time sets the storage limit?

122
00:09:15.934 --> 00:09:18.357 line:85%
Monday at 7:00 a.m., the older milk.

123
00:09:18.987 --> 00:09:21.517 line:85%
Label the combined container with that time.

124
00:09:22.497 --> 00:09:24.537 line:85%
Breast milk doesn’t need to be warmed.

125
00:09:25.167 --> 00:09:30.001 line:85%
It can be served at room temperature or cold, if that’s what the family prefers.

126
00:09:30.981 --> 00:09:33.340 line:85%
If you warm it, keep the container sealed.

127
00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:38.254 line:85%
Place it in a bowl of warm water, or hold it under warm, but not hot,

128
00:09:38.284 --> 00:09:40.255 line:85%
running water for a few minutes.

129
00:09:40.885 --> 00:09:44.460 line:85%
Test the temperature with a few drops on your wrist before feeding.

130
00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:48.887 line:85%
Never heat breast milk on the stove or in a microwave.

131
00:09:49.517 --> 00:09:53.562 line:85%
Microwaves can create hot spots that can burn a baby’s mouth.

132
00:09:54.192 --> 00:09:57.533 line:85%
And once milk is warmed, the 2 hour clock has started.

133
00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:00.882 line:85%
Now formula.

134
00:10:01.512 --> 00:10:03.039 line:85%
It comes in three forms.

135
00:10:04.019 --> 00:10:06.848 line:85%
Ready to feed formula is used as it is.

136
00:10:07.478 --> 00:10:09.048 line:85%
Don’t add any water to it.

137
00:10:10.028 --> 00:10:15.097 line:85%
Liquid concentrate and powder are mixed with water, but only as the label directs.

138
00:10:16.077 --> 00:10:19.609 line:85%
Wash your hands well, and clean and sanitize the workspace.

139
00:10:20.239 --> 00:10:22.577 line:85%
Bottles need to be clean and sanitized.

140
00:10:23.207 --> 00:10:26.612 line:85%
If you use powdered formula, use water from a safe source.

141
00:10:27.242 --> 00:10:30.026 line:85%
If the family isn’t sure their tap water is safe,

142
00:10:30.056 --> 00:10:32.609 line:85%
they can contact the local health department.

143
00:10:33.589 --> 00:10:34.797 line:85%
Measure exactly.

144
00:10:35.427 --> 00:10:39.918 line:85%
Use the amount of water listed on the container, and measure the water first,

145
00:10:39.948 --> 00:10:41.093 line:85%
then add the powder.

146
00:10:41.723 --> 00:10:46.045 line:85%
Too much water may mean the formula won’t meet the baby’s nutritional needs.

147
00:10:46.675 --> 00:10:51.231 line:85%
Too little can make the baby’s kidneys and digestive system work too hard.

148
00:10:52.211 --> 00:10:54.998 line:85%
Never water formula down or make it stronger,

149
00:10:55.028 --> 00:10:58.816 line:85%
unless the baby’s clinician has given different instructions.

150
00:10:59.796 --> 00:11:01.025 line:85%
Let’s read a label.

151
00:11:01.655 --> 00:11:04.547 line:85%
This is a teaching label, not a real product.

152
00:11:05.177 --> 00:11:10.160 line:85%
It says: pour 4 fluid ounces of water into the bottle, then add 2 unpacked,

153
00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:11.697 line:85%
level scoops of powder.

154
00:11:12.327 --> 00:11:14.302 line:85%
What goes in first, and how much?

155
00:11:14.932 --> 00:11:16.118 line:85%
Pause and answer.

156
00:11:36.148 --> 00:11:41.110 line:85%
Water first, exactly 4 fluid ounces, then exactly 2 level scoops.

157
00:11:41.740 --> 00:11:46.391 line:85%
Every product is different, so always follow the label on the container in front of

158
00:11:46.421 --> 00:11:46.617 line:85%
you.

159
00:11:47.597 --> 00:11:53.131 line:85%
Some babies need extra precautions with powdered formula: babies younger than 2

160
00:11:53.161 --> 00:11:56.583 line:85%
months, babies born prematurely, before 37 weeks,

161
00:11:56.613 --> 00:11:59.471 line:85%
and babies with a weakened immune system.

162
00:12:00.451 --> 00:12:05.818 line:85%
Powdered formula isn’t sterile and can sometimes contain germs such as Cronobacter.

163
00:12:06.448 --> 00:12:09.362 line:85%
Infections are rare, but serious in infants.

164
00:12:10.342 --> 00:12:13.303 line:85%
For these babies, CDC says to boil the water,

165
00:12:13.333 --> 00:12:17.224 line:85%
then wait about 5 minutes before mixing it with the powder.

166
00:12:17.854 --> 00:12:23.797 line:85%
Very hot water, around 158°F, is needed to kill these germs.

167
00:12:24.777 --> 00:12:29.057 line:85%
After mixing, the formula is too hot to feed, so let it cool first.

168
00:12:29.687 --> 00:12:31.449 line:85%
Test a few drops on your wrist.

169
00:12:32.079 --> 00:12:33.926 line:85%
It should feel warm, not hot.

170
00:12:34.906 --> 00:12:39.801 line:85%
If a baby has a clinician directed feeding plan, or uses a special formula,

171
00:12:39.831 --> 00:12:43.281 line:85%
follow those instructions and the product directions.

172
00:12:43.911 --> 00:12:45.353 line:85%
Ask before you assume.

173
00:12:46.333 --> 00:12:50.002 line:85%
Keep an opened container of powdered formula in a cool,

174
00:12:50.032 --> 00:12:54.239 line:85%
dry place with the lid tightly closed, not in the refrigerator.

175
00:12:54.869 --> 00:12:58.418 line:85%
Check the label for how long it can be used after opening,

176
00:12:58.448 --> 00:13:01.133 line:85%
and never use formula after its Use By date.

177
00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:04.887 line:85%
Now the two milk clocks.

178
00:13:05.517 --> 00:13:09.612 line:85%
Prepared formula and expressed breast milk run on different events,

179
00:13:09.642 --> 00:13:11.951 line:85%
so we keep them on separate timelines.

180
00:13:12.931 --> 00:13:16.479 line:85%
Prepared formula: use it within 2 hours of preparation,

181
00:13:16.509 --> 00:13:19.536 line:85%
and within 1 hour from when the feeding begins.

182
00:13:20.166 --> 00:13:25.032 line:85%
If you won’t start using it within 2 hours, store it in the fridge right away

183
00:13:25.062 --> 00:13:26.749 line:85%
and use it within 24 hours.

184
00:13:27.729 --> 00:13:31.326 line:85%
And throw out any formula left in the bottle after the feed.

185
00:13:31.956 --> 00:13:35.766 line:85%
Formula and the baby’s saliva can cause bacteria to grow.

186
00:13:36.746 --> 00:13:37.697 line:85%
Worked case.

187
00:13:38.327 --> 00:13:40.388 line:85%
Formula is prepared at 10:00 p.m.

188
00:13:41.018 --> 00:13:42.716 line:85%
That sets a limit of midnight.

189
00:13:43.346 --> 00:13:45.449 line:85%
The feed starts at 10:40 p.m.

190
00:13:46.079 --> 00:13:48.673 line:85%
That sets a limit of 11:40 p.m.

191
00:13:49.303 --> 00:13:53.497 line:85%
The feed ends at 11:05 p.m. with some formula left.

192
00:13:54.127 --> 00:13:55.207 line:85%
When does it go?

193
00:14:15.237 --> 00:14:17.980 line:85%
When the feed ends, at 11:05 p.m.

194
00:14:18.610 --> 00:14:21.183 line:85%
Leftover formula is discarded then.

195
00:14:21.813 --> 00:14:24.812 line:85%
The other limits don’t turn it into a bottle for later.

196
00:14:25.792 --> 00:14:27.277 line:85%
Expressed breast milk.

197
00:14:27.907 --> 00:14:32.058 line:85%
Once it’s brought to room temperature or warmed, use it within 2 hours.

198
00:14:32.688 --> 00:14:37.755 line:85%
If the baby doesn’t finish, the leftover milk can be used within 2 hours after the

199
00:14:37.785 --> 00:14:39.122 line:85%
baby finishes feeding.

200
00:14:39.752 --> 00:14:41.471 line:85%
After that, it’s thrown away.

201
00:14:42.451 --> 00:14:43.616 line:85%
Now an overlap.

202
00:14:44.246 --> 00:14:46.477 line:85%
A bottle is warmed at 11:00 p.m.

203
00:14:47.107 --> 00:14:49.317 line:85%
That warming clock ends at 1:00 a.m.

204
00:14:49.947 --> 00:14:52.349 line:85%
The feed ends at 11:30 p.m.

205
00:14:52.979 --> 00:14:55.723 line:85%
That leftover clock ends at 1:30 a.m.

206
00:14:56.353 --> 00:14:57.816 line:85%
Which deadline applies?

207
00:15:17.846 --> 00:15:21.016 line:85%
In this course we apply the earlier deadline: 1:00 a.m.

208
00:15:21.646 --> 00:15:25.157 line:85%
A later event doesn’t restart a clock that’s already running.

209
00:15:25.787 --> 00:15:30.579 line:85%
That’s how we combine the two CDC limits, and it’s marked for clinical review.

210
00:15:31.559 --> 00:15:33.065 line:85%
One more difference to know.

211
00:15:33.695 --> 00:15:38.760 line:85%
AAP’s HealthyChildren page says leftover milk may be quickly refrigerated

212
00:15:38.790 --> 00:15:40.854 line:85%
and used for the next feeding.

213
00:15:41.484 --> 00:15:46.169 line:85%
CDC says leftover milk is used within 2 hours, then thrown away.

214
00:15:46.799 --> 00:15:49.179 line:85%
This course teaches the CDC rule.

215
00:15:49.809 --> 00:15:53.332 line:85%
If the family’s pediatrician gives different instructions,

216
00:15:53.362 --> 00:15:55.966 line:85%
follow the family’s plan and write it down.

217
00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:01.733 line:85%
It’s 2:00 a.m. on Wednesday, and the baby is cueing to feed.

218
00:16:02.363 --> 00:16:04.039 line:85%
These are teaching examples.

219
00:16:05.019 --> 00:16:10.284 line:85%
The family’s plan: offer expressed milk from the fridge, use labeled milk only,

220
00:16:10.314 --> 00:16:13.501 line:85%
and if no labeled milk is usable, wake a parent.

221
00:16:14.481 --> 00:16:17.407 line:85%
Container A is a bag expressed Sunday, frozen,

222
00:16:17.437 --> 00:16:19.849 line:85%
then moved to the fridge Monday night.

223
00:16:20.479 --> 00:16:24.353 line:85%
The label says it was completely thawed Tuesday at 4:00 p.m.

224
00:16:25.333 --> 00:16:28.887 line:85%
Container B is a bottle warmed at 11:20 p.m.

225
00:16:29.517 --> 00:16:33.199 line:85%
The baby fed from it until 11:45 p.m.

226
00:16:33.829 --> 00:16:35.740 line:85%
It has been on the nightstand since.

227
00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:40.914 line:85%
Container C is a bottle of milk in the fridge door, with no label.

228
00:16:41.894 --> 00:16:45.917 line:85%
For each one, choose use, discard, or set aside and verify.

229
00:16:46.547 --> 00:16:48.011 line:85%
Name the rule you used.

230
00:16:48.641 --> 00:16:52.152 line:85%
Then write one sentence you’d say to the parent in the morning.

231
00:16:52.782 --> 00:16:54.928 line:85%
Pause the video if you need more time.

232
00:18:24.958 --> 00:18:26.656 line:85%
Container A: use it.

233
00:18:27.286 --> 00:18:30.522 line:85%
It was completely thawed Tuesday at 4:00 p.m.,

234
00:18:30.552 --> 00:18:35.917 line:85%
so it can be used until Wednesday at 4:00 p.m., 24 hours from complete thaw.

235
00:18:36.547 --> 00:18:38.992 line:85%
Once you warm it, the 2 hour clock starts.

236
00:18:39.972 --> 00:18:41.819 line:85%
Container B: discard it.

237
00:18:42.449 --> 00:18:48.691 line:85%
The warming clock ended at 1:20 a.m., and the leftover clock ended at 1:45 a.m.

238
00:18:49.321 --> 00:18:50.422 line:85%
Both have passed.

239
00:18:51.402 --> 00:18:54.188 line:85%
Container C: set it aside and verify.

240
00:18:54.818 --> 00:18:59.457 line:85%
You can’t confirm which baby it’s for, what it contains, or how it was handled,

241
00:18:59.487 --> 00:19:01.231 line:85%
and you can’t tell by looking.

242
00:19:01.861 --> 00:19:06.417 line:85%
You have a usable labeled bag, so there’s no need to wake anyone for this one.

243
00:19:07.397 --> 00:19:09.053 line:85%
Now say your update out loud.

244
00:19:24.083 --> 00:19:25.610 line:85%
Here’s one way to say it.

245
00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:27.598 line:85%
At 2:00 a.m.

246
00:19:28.228 --> 00:19:30.758 line:85%
I used the bag that thawed Tuesday afternoon.

247
00:19:31.388 --> 00:19:36.819 line:85%
I threw out the bottle from the 11:20 p.m. feed because it was past 2 hours.

248
00:19:37.449 --> 00:19:39.915 line:85%
There’s an unlabeled bottle in the fridge door.

249
00:19:40.545 --> 00:19:42.649 line:85%
I set it aside so you can check it.

250
00:19:43.579 --> 00:19:48.093 line:85%
That’s the skill: what’s in it, what has happened to it, and which deadline applies.

251
00:19:49.073 --> 00:19:51.325 line:85%
Keep the decision sheet with you on shift.

252
00:19:51.955 --> 00:19:56.789 line:85%
When you’ve completed the required viewing, take the untimed five question quiz.

253
00:19:57.419 --> 00:20:00.653 line:85%
Four correct answers earn your Certificate of Completion.

254
00:20:01.283 --> 00:20:03.984 line:85%
Clickable sources are in the course resources.
